# Benchmarks, 2026-11-25: a faster VM, and native code for lists and records

<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->

The 25 programs of [`bench/compute`](../bench/compute/), each written in CigScript and again in Python (all 25), JavaScript (24: all but `csv`), Lua (21) and Rust (8), timed side by side on one machine, a day after [the first run with stage `c`](BENCHMARKS-2026-11-24.md). Two things changed in that day, and this run measures both:

- **cig's VM runs bytecode faster.** A call between two functions none of whose variables a closure captures is made beside the VM's fast loop, without leaving it for the general path; the loop reads instructions of four bytes; and a run reads what was added to the run index since the last one, not all of it ([#252](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/issues/252)).
- **Stage `c` compiles more of a script.** [cigcompile](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-compile) gives native code to a loop over a list, to `push` and to a record's fields, which it reads and writes where they lie; and an instruction it has no machine code for costs one call of the runtime, not a trip through the interpreter's loop.

**This is a run of cig's source, not of a release.** None of it is in a release yet, so cig is built here from commit `ed7ad3a` and cigcompile from commit `b0a287b`, cig as its release workflow builds it for Linux (`cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`). It is the machine of the run of 2026-11-24, on the same core, with the same harness, the same programs and the same builds of Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust, so [one section](#since-the-last-paper) sets the two runs side by side. Within this one, every side ran in the same rounds.

Every number of the comparison is made from the samples in [`bench/results/2026-11-25/`](../bench/results/2026-11-25/) by [`bench/compare/report.py`](../bench/compare/report.py), and made again by [`bench/compare/check.py`](../bench/compare/check.py), a second program written from the [specification](../bench/compare/SPEC.md) alone; the two agree on every one.

## In short

- **A stage `c` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time** at the geometric mean of the {{n.compute.stage-c.lua}} compute programs both have: faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.lua}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-c.lua}}. A day earlier it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-c.lua}} times. Against CPython 3.12 it takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.python}} of the time (it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-c.python}}; faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.python}} of {{n.compute.stage-c.python}} programs), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit-off}} times LuaJIT's interpreter's ({{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit-off}}), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit}} times LuaJIT's with its compiler on ({{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit}}), and {{geomean.compute.stage-c.rust}} times Rust's over the {{n.compute.stage-c.rust}} programs Rust has ({{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-c.rust}}). Node 18 takes {{median.hello.node}} ms to start on this machine, which decides its row ({{geomean.compute.stage-c.node}}).
- **The bytecode alone got faster.** A stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}}, and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}} of CPython's, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:geomean.compute.stage-b.python}}.
- **Records and lists are native.** `nbody`, whose loops read and write records, takes {{ratio.nbody.stage-c.stage-b}} of its stage `b` time, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:ratio.nbody.stage-c.stage-b}} (it had no native code); `sieve`, whose loops walk a list, {{ratio.sieve.stage-c.stage-b}}, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:ratio.sieve.stage-c.stage-b}}. The loops of arithmetic were native already: `mandel` {{ratio.mandel.stage-c.stage-b}}, `fib` {{ratio.fib.stage-c.stage-b}}, `fannkuch` {{ratio.fannkuch.stage-c.stage-b}} and `loop` {{ratio.loop.stage-c.stage-b}}, each against a stage `b` program that is itself faster than it was.
- **Over all {{n.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} compute programs a stage `c` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} of stage `b`'s time**: faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, level (within 3%) on {{level.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}. {{n.native.stage-c.stage-b}} programs have native code now ({{earlier:2026-11-24:n.native.stage-c.stage-b}} had); the ones it does not speed up are loops of strings, of dictionary lookups and of calls, where one instruction's work is the runtime's either way. The one that comes out slower, `json-deep`, has no native code at all: its two programs are the same file ([below](#stage-c-against-stage-b)).
- **A stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of `cig run`'s time**, and a stage `c` program {{geomean.compute.stage-c.cig}}.
- **Start-up is half what it was.** `hello` takes {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms as a stage `b` program, {{median.hello.stage-c}} as a stage `c` one and {{median.hello.cig}} under `cig run`, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:median.hello.stage-b}}, {{earlier:2026-11-24:median.hello.stage-c}} and {{earlier:2026-11-24:median.hello.cig}}. CPython takes {{median.hello.python}} ms, Lua {{median.hello.lua}} and Node {{median.hello.node}}.

## Since the last paper

Each side's median time in this run divided by its median in [the run of 2026-11-24](BENCHMARKS-2026-11-24.md). Under 1, the side took less time than it did a day earlier.

{{table:since:2026-11-24}}

- **The other languages are the yardstick.** Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust ran from the same binaries on the same core in both runs, and nothing about them changed: their columns say how far two runs of one thing differ on this machine: within four hundredths wherever the program takes five milliseconds or more, and by more than a tenth on some that take two. Their geometric means are in the last row. What cig's columns show beyond that is cig's.
- **Start-up.** The four start-up programs take about half the time on every cig side: `hello` under `cig run` took {{earlier:2026-11-24:median.hello.cig}} ms and takes {{median.hello.cig}}. Until now a run read the whole run index before it started; it reads what was appended since the last run.
- **`cig run`** runs its script on the tree-walker, which did not change: its column is that start-up alone, about three milliseconds, which is most of a small program's time and nothing in a long one's (`trees` {{median.trees.cig}} ms, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-24:median.trees.cig}}).
- **Calls and the fast loop** are the rest of what the stage `b` column gained: `fib`, `spectral`, `trees`, `closures` and `dispatch` are calls, and the loops of `mandel`, `loop` and `fannkuch` run on the four-byte instructions.
- **Stage `c`** has the VM's gains under it and its own on top: `nbody` and `richards` for records, `sieve` for a loop over a list. `mandel`, `fib`, `loop` and `fannkuch` were native already, and most of what they gained is the start-up.

## Stage c against stage b

Each program's median time as a stage `b` program and as a stage `c` program, the second divided by the first, and what of the program cigcompile gave native code: its top level, so many of its functions, or nothing. The last two rows are the geometric mean of the ratios over every compute program and over the ones with native code, with how many were faster, level (within 3%) and slower.

{{table:native}}

**What gets native code.** `cigcompile build --stage c` compiles a script's bytecode loop by loop to machine code that runs on the same registers cig's VM uses, so native code can sit beside anything: what it has no machine code for, the runtime runs.

- **A loop of arithmetic** on ints, floats and bools, with a list's elements read and written by an int: `mandel`, `loop`, `fannkuch`, `sieve`.
- **A loop over a list** (`for x in xs`), and `push`: the list's elements are read where they lie, and an element that owns memory (a string, a list, a record) is put in its register by the runtime.
- **A record's fields.** A map a script writes as a record has a shape, the runtime keeps for each field instruction the shape it last saw and the field's place, and machine code compares one number and reads or writes the value there: `nbody` {{ratio.nbody.stage-c.stage-b}}, `richards` {{ratio.richards.stage-c.stage-b}}.
- **A function on numbers alone**, compiled whole for the argument types it is called with: `fib`.
- **One instruction the runtime runs for it**: a string built or compared, a builtin called, a dictionary read by a computed key. Machine code calls the runtime for that one instruction and goes on, where it used to hand a run of instructions to the interpreter's loop. Such a call costs about what the interpreter takes for the instruction, so a loop made of them is compiled and comes out level or a little ahead: `strings` {{ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}}, `words` {{ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}, `regex-ish` {{ratio.regex-ish.stage-c.stage-b}}, `maps` {{ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, `lists` {{ratio.lists.stage-c.stage-b}}.
- **Not yet:** a loop whose work is calls (`closures`, `dispatch`, `trees`, and the inner loops of `spectral`, whose three functions stay bytecode under a native top level: {{ratio.spectral.stage-c.stage-b}}). A call from native code goes through the VM, which costs more than the interpreter making the call itself, so the compiler leaves such a loop to the bytecode. Nor a type for what a field or an element holds: every `+` on a value read from one still tests its operands.

**None is slower by more than the run can tell.** By its own figures a stage `c` program is never slower than its stage `b` program. One came out past the 3% line here: `json-deep`, at {{ratio.json-deep.stage-c.stage-b}}. It has no native code: its stage `b` and stage `c` programs are one file (one sha256 in [`inputs.tsv`](../bench/results/2026-11-25/pass1/inputs.tsv)), run in the same rounds, so its ratio says what two copies of one program of three milliseconds can differ by in this run. Timed alone with `deltablue` for {{earlier:alone:rounds.pass1}} rounds under each of two other seeds ([`alone/`](../bench/results/2026-11-25/alone/)), the same two files come out at {{earlier:alone:ratio.json-deep.stage-c.stage-b}}, the other way round, and `deltablue`, whose native top level saves it less than a run of two milliseconds can show, at {{earlier:alone:ratio.deltablue.stage-c.stage-b}} ({{ratio.deltablue.stage-c.stage-b}} here).

**What the native code itself adds:** the same stage `c` programs run with `CIG_NATIVE=0`, which makes them run their bytecode and never load their native code, take {{inverse.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}} times as long at the geometric mean (stage `c` ÷ the same program without its native code: {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}}).

## Against each language

A cell is cig's time divided by the other language's: the geometric mean, over the programs both have, of the ratio of their median times. Under 1, cig took less time. In brackets: how many of those programs cig was faster on, level with (within 3%) and slower on.

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **`cig run`** is cig's tree-walking interpreter, as `cig run script.cig` runs a script.
- **A stage `a` program** is what `cigcompile build` makes by default: cig itself with the script inside it, run by the same interpreter.
- **A stage `b` program** (`cigcompile build --stage b`) runs the script's bytecode on cig's VM.
- **A stage `c` program** (`cigcompile build --stage c`) is a stage `b` program with native code beside its bytecode.

These are the {{programs.compute}} programs that are not start-up tests; start-up has [its own section](#start-up).

